#X text 716 223 svfilter2~ implements a trapezoidal-integration (zero-delay feedback) state-variable filter \, following the topology-preserving transform (TPT) approach popularized by Vadim Zavalishin. It is used in Mutable Instruments' Plaits synthesis engine and where I got this code from., f 48;
#X text 714 314 Unlike the classic Chamberlin design (which is what [svfilter~] is) its frequency coefficient is derived from a prewarped tangent function \, keeping cutoff frequency accurate even near Nyquist \, and its resonance parameter maps directly to a true Q value (r = 1/Q) rather than a normalized 0–1 range. The filter is unconditionally stable by construction — increasing Q narrows the resonant peak and lengthens the ring time at cutoff \, without any risk of runaway output \, though there is no built-in saturation \, so extremely high Q values can produce long \, clean \, undamped ringing rather than the softly saturated resonance of svfilter~. Recommended Q range for musical use is roughly 0.5–20., f 69;
#X text 717 27 It implements the Chamberlin state-variable filter topology (as described in Musical Applications of Microprocessors). It is a simple Euler-integrated digital approximation of the classic analog SVF circuit. There's a low-cost (sin()-based frequency coefficient and a small cubic soft-clip is applied to the internal band-pass state on every sample \, which both keeps the filter well-behaved at high resonance and gives it a mildly saturated \, "analog-ish" tone. Because this topology uses straightforward Euler integration rather than a prewarped/bilinear design \, its tuning accuracy degrades somewhat as cutoff frequency approaches Nyquist.;
